WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.570
These ants are working in
tandem to form a bridge.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.730
Even though there's no
leader giving orders,

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.690
they all seem to
know what to do.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:09.660
Similarly, thousands
of starlings

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.230
fly in unison, creating
complex formations in the sky.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.300
Magnetic properties
arise from the alignment

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.130
of billions of electrons.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.160
Intricate frost patterns
crystallize on window panes.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.610
Hurricanes form above
churning oceans,

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:26.070
and once clear highways suddenly
get jammed with traffic.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.720
These different
systems all share

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.320
something in common, some sort
of macro property or behavior

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.520
arises spontaneously from
many interacting micro parts.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.710
It's a classic case of
the whole being greater

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.400
than the sum of its parts.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.370
The behavior of
each of those parts

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.580
doesn't predict how they
behave collectively.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.590
Some of our greatest
unsolved mysteries,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.140
like how life first began,
and how our brains give

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.830
rise to consciousness could be
explained by this phenomenon,

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.544
known as emergence.

00:00:54.544 --> 00:00:56.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.030
How does complexity emerge
from a simple starting point,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.940
and how do we even study that?

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.820
Let's look at the formation
of a crystal lattice.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.670
Think of molecules
in a container.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.350
At high temperatures, they'll
bounce around independently

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.240
in a fluid or gaseous state.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.940
Poke them, and nothing
happens to the system,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.750
but lower the temperature
and they form a solid.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.710
They crystallize,
arranging themselves

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.570
in a regular lattice.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.970
A balancing act
between opposing forces

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.520
maintains the distance
between molecules.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.810
Now, poke them.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.150
They'll try to
restore the balance.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.050
The key is, the forces acting
on the molecules don't change.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.680
The drop in temperature added
a new condition to the system.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.647
The same rules created
a different effect.

00:01:43.647 --> 00:01:45.480
And that's how the
properties of the crystal

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.380
lattice, like its rigidity, its
elasticity, and its long range

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.450
order emerge.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.300
An abrupt change in the
behavior of a physical system

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.760
is something we typically
think of as a phase transition.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.700
In fact, that's how
scientists often

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.790
think about emergence
in more complex systems.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.160
Let's return to the
bridge building ants.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.840
Like the molecules
in the lattice,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.110
the ants as individuals obey
the same behavioral rules,

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.550
no matter what they're doing.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.510
They aren't trying
to build structures.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.110
But when the ground drops
away in front of the swarm,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.690
old rules about how close
to stay to their neighbors

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:23.190
leads to the emergence of large
scale coordinated behavior.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.380
Without that information
about their neighbors,

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.360
the bridge would
break apart, just

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.950
like how higher
temperatures cause

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.790
molecules to break from a solid
lattice into a liquid state.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.910
Emergence means that we can
find hierarchies of new laws

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.980
and structures that arise out of
complexity at different scales.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.740
Scientists are seeking to apply
the framework of emergence

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.430
to other areas of research,
like how individual cells follow

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.000
cues to move or transform
when organs are developing.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.310
Or how groups of
neurons fire together,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.410
to help you make decisions.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.930
Some researchers are
even hoping to engineer

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.230
such systems of their own, to
build robotic systems made up

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.110
of small dumb parts that
end up swarming together

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.340
to perform useful tasks.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.280
Perhaps this work can
help us understand

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.130
even deeper questions
about the emergent nature

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.040
of intelligence,
consciousness, and life itself.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

